WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.350
Data centres burn electricity and evaporate water to keep servers cool.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:12.850
Many jobs are flexible: they could run at another site, or later.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:19.850
GreenQ picks a smarter place and time for each job, with a
quantum algorithm.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.850
Our example: four jobs, two data centres, a day slot and a
night slot.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:34.850
Run now: every job starts at home, the moment it arrives.
Site A gets crowded at night.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:41.850
Now we press run smart. The jobs move to cleaner time slots
and drier cooling.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:47.850
Same jobs: 530 kilograms less CO2 and 1,798 litres less water.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.850
Dewy explains each move, for example the training job.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:00.850
Carbon and water pull against each other.
We drag from cutting carbon to saving water.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:08.850
With carbon only we save 593 kilograms of CO2;
with water only, 2,134 litres of water.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:15.850
This curve shows every compromise. The three IBM marks ran on a
real quantum computer.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:23.850
Here is the real circuit: 8 qubits, one where and one when
for each job.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:31.850
Every measured answer is a valid schedule.
The usual method needs 16 qubits.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:39.350
It ran on IBM's Marrakesh computer:
114 qubit links, 7 runs, every job ID saved.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:47.350
Random guessing puts 10 percent of answers in the best tenth.
IBM's computer put 18 to 23 percent.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:53.850
A perfect simulator reaches 28 to 34 percent.
Noise-reduction tricks did not help consistently.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.350
Now we ask GreenQ a question.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:05.350
The answer comes only from our saved results, and every number is
checked against the data.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.850
Then the honest question.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:18.850
The answer is no, not at this size.
A laptop is faster, so we use it as our referee.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:27.850
Against simple methods like greedy and annealing, all find the best plan
here, and faster.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.350
Bigger problems: 114 to 1,010 qubit links.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:36.850
With job rules, savings shrink but remain.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:41.850
You can also describe your own jobs in plain English.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:49.850
Built with Qiskit on IBM Quantum, Python, FastAPI and React.
Groq writes explanations only.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.350
Same jobs: 530 kilograms less CO2, 1,798 litres less water.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:01.850
Sites are illustrative and we claim no quantum advantage.
Next: bigger problems, real data.
